  <p class="standfirst">MetaMind demonstrates a narrow but consequential fact: even frontier models can improve when inference is organized around <strong>latent states, revision, memory, and validation</strong>. This essay asks what follows if that scaffold becomes a trainable cognitive system.</p>

  <div class="margin-note">
    <strong>Epistemic boundary</strong>
    <p>The reported MetaMind results are empirical. The agenda below is an interpretation and a set of testable research hypotheses — not an additional claim established by the NeurIPS paper. Evidence, inference, and speculation are labeled separately throughout.</p>
  </div>

  <p class="section-kicker">01 / Boundary</p>
  <h2 id="boundary">What the paper proves.<br />What it only suggests.</h2>

  <p>The evidence is specific. A staged, model-agnostic architecture improves Theory-of-Mind reasoning, social cognition, and interactive social performance across diverse backbones. The gains persist on advanced reasoning models; every stage contributes; and expert raters find the revised hypotheses coherent.</p>

  <p>That does <em>not</em> prove that parameter scaling has ended, that language models possess human cognition, or that one architecture generalizes to every cognitive domain. It does support a more disciplined proposition: <strong>how inference is organized remains a meaningful axis of capability, even after pretraining.</strong></p>

  <section class="evidence-ribbon" aria-label="Evidence to agenda boundary">
    <div><strong>Observed</strong><span>external cognitive structure improves social reasoning</span></div>
    <div><strong>Inferred</strong><span>latent capability may be under-elicited by direct decoding</span></div>
    <div><strong>Hypothesized</strong><span>the useful process can be trained and internalized</span></div>
    <div><strong>Tested next</strong><span>interaction, worlds, and long-horizon consequence</span></div>
  </section>

