  <p class="section-kicker">02 / Thesis</p>
  <h2 id="thesis">The next curve is not<br />only a scaling curve.</h2>

  <p>Pretraining compresses vast regularities about language, behavior, and the world into a model. Direct decoding asks that model to retrieve, interpret, deliberate, align, and act inside one undifferentiated pass. MetaMind instead inserts a control structure: first form hypotheses, then apply constraints, then validate the action.</p>

  <p>The fact that o3 still improves from 90.3 to 92.2 under that structure matters. It suggests that stronger models can remain <em>procedurally under-specified</em>: they may know more than the default inference path reliably uses. Scaling remains essential, but scale and cognitive organization are not substitutes. They are orthogonal levers.</p>

  <section class="research-line" aria-label="Research program from scaffold to world">
    <ol>
      <li><span>Externalize</span><strong>Scaffold</strong><p>Make the hidden cognitive loop explicit and inspectable.</p></li>
      <li><span>Measure</span><strong>Instrument</strong><p>Evaluate process quality, not only final-answer correctness.</p></li>
      <li><span>Internalize</span><strong>Learn</strong><p>Distill the successful process into a lower-cost policy or latent state.</p></li>
      <li><span>Ground</span><strong>World</strong><p>Test whether cognition survives interaction and consequence over time.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <p>This sequence connects a visible line of work: MetaMind externalizes social cognition; Cognition-of-Thought makes the process auditable during decoding; the next step is to internalize that process through reinforcement learning; MarioLM and long-horizon evaluation then ask whether the learned cognition changes trajectories rather than isolated answers.</p>

  <p class="section-kicker">03 / Principles</p>
  <h2 id="principles">Three principles for<br />cognitive system design.</h2>

  <div class="agenda-grid">
    <article><b>01</b><span>Cognition as scaffold</span><h3>Constrain the shape, not the conclusion.</h3><p>A useful scaffold specifies which latent variables to consider and when to revise them, while leaving the model free to generate the actual hypotheses. Structure supplies an inductive bias without hard-coding the answer.</p></article>
    <article><b>02</b><span>Cognition as protocol</span><h3>Separate generation, critique, and action.</h3><p>The infer → constrain → validate loop is a reusable protocol for partially observed problems. Its value lies in distinct interfaces where a system can expose uncertainty and accept correction.</p></article>
    <article><b>03</b><span>Cognition as learned policy</span><h3>Use the scaffold, then earn the right to remove it.</h3><p>External multi-agent computation is expensive. The long-term goal is to supervise, distill, or reinforce its successful internal transitions until the capability becomes native and efficient.</p></article>
    <article><b>04</b><span>Cognition as state</span><h3>Remember what changed, not only what was said.</h3><p>Interaction requires persistent beliefs about goals, trust, and constraints. Memory should track revisable latent state rather than accumulate unstructured transcripts.</p></article>
  </div>

  <div class="media-pair">
    <figure><img src="/blog/MetaMind/assets/c_scaffold.webp" width="1328" height="1328" alt="A branching structure supported by scaffolding, representing structured reasoning" loading="lazy" /><figcaption><strong>Scaffold.</strong> Explicit structure can stabilize inference before it is internalized.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure><img src="/blog/MetaMind/assets/c_process_reward.webp" width="1328" height="1328" alt="A luminous branching trajectory, representing process-level reward" loading="lazy" /><figcaption><strong>Process.</strong> In ambiguous domains, learning must evaluate trajectories of thought and action, not only terminal labels.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Self-play becomes self-involvement</h3>
  <p>Multi-agent debate is often treated as a coordination trick. Social cognition makes a deeper version possible: an agent models another agent who is simultaneously modeling it. That recursive loop can generate interaction data, expose strategic blind spots, and turn the model from a spectator into a participant.</p>

  <p>The opportunity is also a risk. Self-generated social experience can amplify shared assumptions, simulate people badly, or optimize persuasion without genuine understanding. A credible program therefore needs external human calibration, uncertainty-aware mental-state models, and explicit limits on how inferred states are used.</p>

  <p class="section-kicker">04 / Transitions</p>
  <h2 id="frontier">Four transitions beyond<br />today's default pipeline.</h2>

  <div class="result-grid">
    <article class="result-card"><span>Transition 01 / Scale → structure</span><strong>Organize</strong><h3>Beyond undifferentiated inference</h3><p>Ask which cognitive operation should occur at each stage, which state it changes, and how the system knows when to revise.</p></article>
    <article class="result-card"><span>Transition 02 / Tokens → latent state</span><strong>Infer</strong><h3>Beyond language as the full input</h3><p>Language is a partial observation. Beliefs, goals, emotion, trust, and role become uncertain state variables that must be updated over time.</p></article>
    <article class="result-card"><span>Transition 03 / Labels → process</span><strong>Evaluate</strong><h3>Beyond verifiable terminal reward</h3><p>Many human outcomes have no single correct answer. Reward must address calibration, empathy, coherence, reversibility, and downstream effect.</p></article>
    <article class="result-card"><span>Transition 04 / Benchmarks → worlds</span><strong>Experience</strong><h3>Beyond static question answering</h3><p>A system's intelligence should be measured by the world its actions produce across turns, people, counterfactuals, and delayed consequences.</p></article>
  </div>

