  <h3>Process reward in domains without a single answer</h3>
  <p>Reinforcement learning with verifiable reward is powerful where correctness can be checked. Social intelligence, education, negotiation, and care do not usually offer that luxury. A response may be locally empathetic yet globally manipulative; immediately helpful yet destructive to autonomy; coherent now yet inconsistent five turns later.</p>

  <p>MetaMind's empathy-and-coherence audit is only a first approximation, but it points toward a richer unit of optimization: the quality of a cognitive and interaction trajectory. Future rewards should combine uncertainty calibration, norm sensitivity, user agency, causal consequence, and the ability to recover after a mistaken inference.</p>

  <p class="section-kicker">05 / Worlds</p>
  <h2 id="worlds">A mind model becomes useful<br />when the world pushes back.</h2>

  <p>Once intent and belief are modeled as latent state, an interaction can be treated as a causal process. The system chooses an action, observes how the person and environment respond, updates its state estimate, and plans again. Social reasoning then becomes a form of partially observed world modeling.</p>

  <figure class="essay-figure">
    <div class="aspect-media"><img src="/blog/MetaMind/assets/c_simulation.webp" width="1664" height="928" alt="A central cognitive model branching into multiple interactive scenes" loading="lazy" /></div>
    <figcaption><strong>From interpretation to simulation.</strong> Candidate mental states induce candidate futures; interaction provides evidence that can confirm or overturn the model.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>Toward causal interaction worlds</h3>
  <p>The immediate step is not a grand simulated civilization. It is a controlled environment where actions produce persistent, measurable effects on latent variables such as trust, agency, cooperation, and goal satisfaction. This is the logic behind experiential evaluation: an answer is judged by the trajectory it creates.</p>

  <h3>Toward future simulation</h3>
  <p>A model that can represent another mind can roll forward several plausible responses before acting. Counterfactual branches — “If I push here, does trust decay later?” — create a bridge between Theory of Mind and planning. MarioLM extends this idea by making causal scenes, event chains, and long-horizon rubrics part of the evaluation world.</p>

  <h3>Toward super-world games</h3>
  <p>The farthest hypothesis is a cognitive world populated by agents that develop social knowledge through play, conflict, cooperation, and mutual modeling. Such worlds could become data engines and scientific instruments for studying emergent behavior. They could also become closed feedback loops that mistake simulation for humanity. The proposal is valuable only if its outputs are continuously tested against real people and real consequences.</p>

  <figure class="essay-figure">
    <div class="aspect-media"><img src="/blog/MetaMind/assets/c_superworld.webp" width="1664" height="928" alt="A figure looking across nested translucent worlds, representing layered cognitive simulation" loading="lazy" /></div>
    <figcaption><strong>Speculative frontier.</strong> Nested cognitive worlds are a research instrument, not a claim that simulation alone can recover human values.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="margin-note">
    <strong>Intellectual lineage</strong>
    <p>This agenda sits near computational Theory of Mind and inverse planning associated with Joshua Tenenbaum; probabilistic cognition and pragmatic inference associated with Noah Goodman; and developmental accounts of social learning through interaction associated with Michael C. Frank. The goal is not to borrow names as decoration, but to make AI system design answer to cognitive theories that can be tested.</p>
  </div>

  <p class="section-kicker">06 / Tests</p>
  <h2 id="tests">A frontier becomes science<br />when it can fail.</h2>

  <p>The Cognitive AI agenda should not be protected by its ambition. Each transition needs a result that could force us to abandon or revise it.</p>

  <ul class="question-list">
    <li><span>01</span><strong>If structured scaffolds stop helping under leakage-controlled, cross-cultural, multimodal tests, the claimed generality is false.</strong></li>
    <li><span>02</span><strong>If the external process cannot be distilled without losing calibration and safety, native cognition may not follow from visible cognition.</strong></li>
    <li><span>03</span><strong>If simulated mental states fail to predict real human responses, the world model is a narrative engine rather than a causal model.</strong></li>
    <li><span>04</span><strong>If personalization improves benchmark utility while reducing autonomy, privacy, or contestability, the alignment objective is wrong.</strong></li>
    <li><span>05</span><strong>If long-horizon evaluation does not change model selection or training outcomes, experiential worlds add spectacle rather than information.</strong></li>
  </ul>

  <p>The immediate program is therefore concrete: reproduce the scaffold across domains; expose and score its intermediate states; learn lower-cost latent policies from successful traces; and test those policies inside interaction worlds with delayed, human-grounded consequences.</p>

  <p>MetaMind is not the destination. It is a useful first instrument — one that makes a hidden process visible enough to study, criticize, train, and eventually replace with something more capable.</p>
